{"type":"assistant","message":{"type":"message","model":"kimi-k3","usage":{"input_tokens":31494,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_5_m_tokens":0,"claude_cache_creation_1_h_tokens":0},"role":"assistant","id":"021a6ae527160a7a91ecb1386a9b6ac5","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Workspace is empty. Now let me get a screenshot of the media library to see what assets look like, and screenshot of components for reference. Actually, to save tool calls, I'll skip detailed screenshots and just use instances of the supplied components.\n\nPlan:\n1. Load fonts (Inter Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold).\n2. Build a set of reusable components in workspace: header, tab bar (5 tabs with variants), stock row, wishlist row, index card, number pad, code input, payment method row, profile row, etc. Actually, to keep the tool-call count manageable, I can build screens directly with helper functions in a few big code blocks, while creating a few key reusable components (TabBar, StatusBar usage from reference, Button instance reuse, Text Field instance reuse).\n\nNote: Components from other pages can be instanced via figma.getNodeByIdAsync and createInstance — that works cross-page within same file. The brief says \"Reuse the components in 02 · Components\" and \"Use the StockWave logo... from 04 · Media\". So I should instantiate those components.\n\nComponent IDs:\n- Button: 4:168\n- Text Field: 4:174\n- Status Bar: 4:180\n- Home Indicator: 4:191\n- StockWave_Symbol: 3:92\n- StockWave_Wordmark: 3:95\n- Logo_Adobe: 3:101, Logo_Amazon: 3:104, Logo_Apple: 3:107, Logo_Bank_of_America: 3:110, Logo_Facebook: 3:113, Logo_Netflix: 3:116\n- Avatar_Neutral: 3:122\n- Blank_Phone_Frame: 3:125\n- Welcome_Hand: 3:134\n- Icon_Apple_Pay: 3:140, Icon_Balance_Visibility: 3:143, Icon_Card: 3:146, Icon_Home: 3:149, Icon_Market: 3:152, Icon_Notifications: 3:155, Icon_Portfolio: 3:158, Icon_Profile: 3:161, Icon_Trade: 3:164\n\nDesign system:\n- Primary: #3E52C1\n- Text primary: #171A1F (0.09,0.10,0.12), secondary: #6B707A (0.42,0.44,0.48)\n- Background: #FFFFFF, surface gray: #F5F6F8\n- Gain: green #12B76A, Loss: red #F04438\n- Border: #E5E7EB\n- Radius 12, buttons 52 tall.\n\nScreens (375×812 each), layout positions in workspace — grid with labels. Frames:\n01_Splash, 02_Onboarding, 03_Sign_Up_Empty, 03_Sign_Up_Filled, 04_Verify_Phone, 05_Verify_Code, 06_Welcome, 07_Home, 08_Portfolio, 09_Stock_Detail, 10_Confirm_Order, 11_Profile.\n\nI'll arrange them in 2 rows of 6, spaced 80px apart, starting at (0,0).\n\nLet me write the build in a few use_figma calls:\n\nCall 1: Setup — load fonts, define helpers, create design-token frame? Skip tokens frame; just build. Actually creating components for reuse is evaluated (\"Component reuse\"). I'll create a components area in workspace with: TabBar component (with 5 variants? simpler: one TabBar component built per screen via a function, but evaluation wants reusable components). To balance tool-call budget, I'll create a few master components in the workspace page: \"Main Navigation\" component with property current tab? Variants would be ideal but complex. Simpler: create component \"Nav/TabBar\" as a component, then for each screen create an instance and override colors of icons/labels per current tab. Overrides on instance children are allowed (fill changes). That demonstrates reuse.\n\nAlso create components: \"Stock Row\", \"Header\" maybe. Time is limited; I'll make TabBar, StockRow, and NumberPad as components. Actually building components then instantiating with overrides costs about the same code as building directly. I'll create:\n\n- Comp: TabBar (component) — with default state (Home active). Instances override active color.\n- Comp: StockRow (component) — logo + ticker + company + sparkline + price + change. Instances override text. Logo override: can't swap instance easily... I can swap nested instances: stockRow contains an instance of Logo_Netflix; override by swapping nested instance via swapComponent? In plugin API, instance children that are instances can be swapped: nestedInstance.swapComponent(otherComponent). That works.\n\nSimpler approach: write a function buildStockRow(parent, opts) that creates a fresh row each time but visually identical — that fails \"component reuse\" evaluation a bit. Compromise: create real components for the most-repeated elements: TabBar, StockRow, WishlistRow, IndexCard, PayRow, ProfileRow, Numpad. That's a lot of work. Given \"Component reuse\" is one of 7 criteria, I should make at least TabBar, StockRow, and a couple others real components.\n\nLet me be pragmatic: I'll write one big setup call that defines all helpers + creates components, then subsequent calls per screen. But each use_figma call runs in a fresh context — helper functions must be redefined each call. That's fine; I'll include a compact helper prelude in each call.\n\nTotal budget: ~12 screens + setup + prototype wiring + screenshots. Tool call limit seems to be rate-limit based (intermittent). I'll keep calls efficient.\n\nFont loading: await figma.loadFontAsync for Inter Regular/Medium/Semi Bold/Bold.\n\nLet me define colors:\nconst C = { primary:{r:0.243,g:0.322,b:0.757}, ink:{r:0.09,g:0.10,b:0.12}, sub:{r:0.42,g:0.44,b:0.48}, faint:{r:0.62,g:0.64,b:0.68}, bg:{r:1,g:1,b:1}, surface:{r:0.965,g:0.969,b:0.98}, line:{r:0.91,g:0.915,b:0.93}, green:{r:0.07,g:0.72,b:0.42}, red:{r:0.94,g:0.27,b:0.22}, greenBg:{r:0.91,g:0.98,b:0.95}, redBg:{r:1,g:0.95,b:0.94}, primaryBg:{r:0.93,g:0.94,b:0.99} }\n\nHelper functions:\n- txt(parent, str, size, weight, color, x, y, opts) — create text node.\n- rect(parent, x,y,w,h, fill, radius)\n- inst(componentId) — find node by id, createInstance.\n\nTrend lines: small polyline via VECTOR. I can create vector with vectorPaths. figma.createVector(); v.vectorPaths = [{windingRule:'NONE', data:'M 0 10 L 10 4 ...'}]; strokeWeight 1.5, stroke color.\n\nFor charts (portfolio bar chart Mon–Sun, NFLX price chart with highlighted point, financial bars 2018-2021): draw with rects/lines.\n\nScreen details:\n\n01_Splash: primary bg? Brand symbol + wordmark centered. Wordmark color may be dark; on primary bg, use white text? The wordmark asset is supplied — place both on white bg, primary-tinted? Splash: white background, symbol above wordmark centered. Simple.\n\n02_Onboarding: Blank_Phone_Frame centered top area, title \"Stock trading suite\", subtitle \"Streamline your investment decisions with expert guidance.\", page dots (2 pages, current 1), Sign up primary button, Sign in secondary/text button, Skip top-right.\n\n03_Sign_Up_Empty: title \"Join StockWave\", subtitle, fields Username/Email/Password (empty), Continue button (primary), \"Or continue with\", Google + Apple buttons (outlined), \"Already have an account? Sign In\".\n\n03_Sign_Up_Filled: same with values: Username \"Agatha Bella\", Email \"agbella@email.com\", Password masked \"••••••••\".\n\nText Field component is 327×52 default empty; I can instance it and override placeholder text for each field. For filled state, set text color to ink and characters to value. The component has a single text node \"Email\" as placeholder. Overriding characters works.\n\n04_Verify_Phone: header back + , title \"Enter your phone number\", subtitle, phone field showing \"+1 202 555 0147\", Send code button, number pad (1-9,*,0,delete).\n\nNumber pad: grid of keys, each 110×56 approx. 375 wide screen: keys ~110px wide with gaps. Standard: 3 columns. Height ~ 4 rows × 64. Place at bottom above home indicator.\n\n05_Verify_Code: back, title \"Enter verification code\", subtitle, 4 code boxes with first digit \"2\" entered, Resend code (link), Verify account button, number pad.\n\n06_Welcome: Welcome_Hand illustration, \"Hello Agatha Bella!\", \"Welcome to StockWave\", \"It's great to have you here\", button \"I'm ready to start!\".\n\n07_Home: status bar, header: StockWave_Wordmark + notifications icon. Portfolio card: \"Portfolio value\", $13,240.11, eye icon (show/hide), 7-day trend line. Indices: two cards (S&P 500 / S&P · Standard & Poor's, +49.50%; Dow Jones DOW +1.43%). Wishlist: \"Wishlist\" + add button, 3 rows (AMZN −0.05%, ADBE +0.32%, FB +1.07%) each logo, ticker, company, change, trend line. Stocks: \"Stocks\", 5 rows (NFLX, AAPL, FB, BAC, F) with logo, ticker, company, price, change, trend. Tab bar current Home. This is long — brief says long screens may scroll vertically. Frame height 812 fixed? \"Long screens (Home, Portfolio, Stock Detail) may scroll vertically\" — I'll keep frame 375×812 but content clipped? For visibility, better make frame taller (e.g. 812 fixed for prototype but content scrolls). For evaluation visibility, I'll make Home frame taller than 812 (e.g., 1400) with tab bar pinned at bottom of visible area... In prototypes, scrolling content needs clipping. Simplest robust approach: keep frames 375×812 and design content to fit compactly. Home has a LOT: header, portfolio card, 2 index cards, wishlist (3 rows), stocks (5 rows). Rows can be compact 56px: 8 rows = 448, portfolio card ~150, indices ~90, headers ~ 120, tab bar 80, status bar 44 → 932. Too much. So make the Home frame taller: 375 × 1180 with a note? But prototype canvas is 812. Alternative: frame 812 with inner scroll — prototype scroll overflow. I can set frame 375×812, add inner content frame taller than 812 with clipContent and overflowDirection vertical → scroll in prototype. Tab bar overlaid fixed. In Figma prototype, \"Scroll\" overflow works. I'll do that: content group with layoutClip... Actually simpler: make the screen frames taller (e.g., Home 812 visible + scrolling). Hmm, evaluation criteria likely inspect frames visually. A taller frame shows everything, which is safer for checklist coverage. But \"Canvas 375 × 812\" is a foundation requirement. Compromise: frames stay 375×812; inner scroll content frame (vertical scroll) holds everything; tab bar fixed at bottom. Set screenFrame.overflowDirection = 'VERTICAL'? For top-level prototype frames, you set overflowDirection on the frame and content exceeds height — prototype shows scroll. Tab bar would scroll too unless I add it as... For simplicity and visibility of all content, I'll instead make the frames taller where needed and mention scrolling in dev... The brief says \"may scroll vertically\", so taller frames representing scrolled content are acceptable-ish, but canvas fixed 375×812 is \"Fixed\".\n\nDecision: Home frame = 375×812 with vertical scroll overflow (clip content, inner content height ~1050, tab bar inside scroll at bottom? No—tab bar should stay). I'll implement: screen frame 812, clips content, overflowDirection VERTICAL; children: contentFrame (auto-layout, full content, height >812) plus absolutely positioned tab bar at bottom (position absolute via constraints or just placed after with higher z and set as \"fixed\" — in Figma, fixed when scrolling requires the node to be outside the scrolling frame... Top-level frame scrolls everything inside. Fixed elements need to be positioned with constraints and marked? In prototyping, a child with `layoutPositioning` absolute inside a scroll frame still scrolls. Fixed position in prototype is only for overlays... Actually Figma supports \"Fix position when scrolling\" — that's set via... plugin API: node.layoutAlign? There's `frameNode.overflowDirection` and for children, `node.constraints`? The \"Fix position when scrolling\" is `node.stuck`? Hmm — in Plugin API there is `children` scroll behavior via \"Scroll with parent\" / \"Fixed\" — property is `node.scrollBehavior`? Actually it's `node.stuckTo`? Let me recall: Figma plugin API has `SceneNode`... I remember `frame.overflowDirection` and `frame.overlayPositionType`. For children: `node.layoutPositioning`... There IS `children` property \"scrollBehavior\": In Figma API: `MINIMAL`? I believe it's `node.scrollBehavior = 'FIXED' | 'SCROLLS'` — hmm, I recall `ScrollBehavior` on `InstanceNode`? There is: `readonly scrollBehavior` on... Actually the Plugin API has `FrameNode` children with `scrollBehavior` property (\"SCROLLS\" | \"FIXED\" | \"STICKY_SCROLL\"?). I'm fairly confident: `node.scrollBehavior = 'FIXED'` exists for children of frames with scrolling. Yes — \"scrollBehavior\" is a property available on children of scrollable frames (added ~2023; values 'SCROLLS' | 'FIXED' | 'STICKY_TOP'...). I'll try setting it; if it errors, catch and ignore.\n\nTo reduce risk: I'll make scrollable screens' content fit by compact design where feasible, and use taller-than-812 only for Home (needs it). Actually let me just do proper scroll: frame 812, inner content ~1100, tab bar fixed. If scrollBehavior fails, catch.\n\n08_Portfolio: back, \"Portfolio\", portfolio value $13,240.11 +1.53% today, bar chart Mon–Sun with Sun highlighted ($13,240.11), Today: Gain $234.11 / Loss $34.11 cards, Stocks NFLX + AAPL rows, tab bar current Portfolio.\n\n09_Stock_Detail: back + \"Stock Detail\" + price alert icon; NFLX logo, NFLX, Netflix Inc; price $88.91, +$1.13 (+1.29%) today; range selector 1D 1W 1M 1Y 5Y ALL current 1M; line chart with highlighted point Sep 27 $88.04; View Summary/Details segmented current Details; Financials: Revenue/Earnings/Performance tabs + bars 2018–2021; Buy stocks button. Long — scroll.\n\n10_Confirm_Order: back + \"Confirm Order\"; order card: logo, NFLX, Netflix Inc, $88.91; rows: Funding source Stockup, Approx share price $88.91, Approx shares 3, Fee $2.10, Total $268.83; Payment method: Debit Card (Icon_Card) \"Deposit & Invest right from your debit card\", Apple Pay (Icon_Apple_Pay) \"Connect your Apple Pay account\", radio unchosen both; Confirm order button.\n\n11_Profile: \"Profile\", Avatar_Neutral, Agatha Bella, agbella@email.com; invite card \"Invite friends\" \"Invite your friends and get $15\"; entries: Account, Security, Billing / Payments, Language (English), Settings, FAQ; tab bar current Profile.\n\nPrototype links:\n- 01 → (auto, after delay) 02: reaction on frame: trigger AFTER_DELAY 800ms navigate 02.\n- 02: Sign up button → 03_Sign_Up_Empty; Skip → 03_Sign_Up_Empty.\n- 03_Filled: Continue → 04.\n- 04: back → 03_Filled; Send code → 05.\n- 05: back → 04; Verify account → 06.\n- 06: I'm ready to start! → 07.\n- 07: Portfolio value card → 08; NFLX row → 09; tabs: Home→07, Portfolio→08, Trade→10, Profile→11 (Market entry only).\n- 08: back → 07; NFLX → 09; tabs.\n- 09: back → 07; Buy stocks → 10.\n- 10: back → 09; Confirm order → 07.\n- 11: tabs.\n\nReactions API: node.reactions = [{trigger:{type:'ON_CLICK'}, actions:[{type:'NODE', destinationId, navigation:'NAVIGATE', transition:{type:'SMART_ANIMATE'...}}]}]. Actually action shape: {type:'NODE', destinationId:'...', navigation:'NAVIGATE', transition:{type:'DISSOLVE', duration:{...}, easing:{type:'...'}}, preserveScrollPosition:false}. For delay: trigger {type:'AFTER_DELAY', timeout:0.8} — delay trigger uses 'timeout' in seconds.\n\nAlso need page prototypeStartPoint? Setting a reaction on 01 with flow starting point: page.prototypeStartPoint? To create a flow, set device... optional. I can set page.prototypeStartPoint? Not directly; flows auto-create from reactions. Fine.\n\nNow — big consideration: tool-call rate limits are intermittent (some failed with limit error, retries worked). I'll write moderately sized calls and retry on failure.\n\nInstance overrides on text inside instances: need to find nested text node: inst.findOne(n => n.type==='TEXT') then loadFont then set characters. Text Field: text child is the placeholder. Button: text child \"Continue\".\n\nCross-page instancing: components live on pages 1:3 and 1:4. createInstance works regardless of page; then append to workspace frame. \n\nFor logo swap in StockRow instances: nested instance swap: nested.swapComponent(figma.getNodeById('3:104')). Works if both are components.\n\nPlan for calls:\n1. Setup call: switch to workspace page, load fonts, store nothing persistent (each call fresh). Create components area: build Component \"TabBar\" with default Home active; Component \"StockRow\"; Component \"WishRow\"? WishRow ~ StockRow variant without price? Wishlist rows have logo, ticker, company, change, trend (no price). Make StockRow include price; WishRow separate component. Component \"NumKey\"? Numpad built per screen (only 2 screens) — build once as component and reuse. Component \"PayRow\", \"ProfileRow\", \"IndexCard\", \"Chip/Range\"? Keep to: TabBar, StockRow, WishRow, NumPad, IndexCard, PayRow, ProfileRow, CodeBox? That's a lot of code. Let me economize: components that repeat ≥2 times: TabBar (3), StockRow (7 rows across 07/08), WishRow (3), NumPad (2), StatusBar/HomeIndicator from reference (instances). IndexCard (2 on home), PayRow (2), ProfileRow (6). I'll make components for TabBar, StockRow, WishRow, NumPad, IndexCard, PayRow, ProfileRow. Good reuse story.\n\nComponents need overrides per instance: text changes (find nested TEXT by name), color changes for tab active states (find icon instance & label by name, change fills — icon instances contain vector shapes; overriding fills of nested vector children is allowed).\n\nIcon tint: icon components are likely colored shapes. To set active/inactive, override fills of all vector descendants to primary/gray. Icon_Trade is white per label \"(white)\" — on white background it'd be invisible; put it in a primary circle? The trade tab often is a primary circular button. I'll design: Trade tab = primary filled circle with white trade icon. \n\nNumpad: component with 12 keys; delete key — draw an icon (backspace) with vector or text \"⌫\"? Use text \"⌫\" symbol, fine. Keys as text on transparent bg.\n\nLayout: screens grid: row1: 01-06 at y=0; row2: 07-12 at y=1000. x spacing: i*455.\n\nLet me now write the setup + components call. I must keep each call's code under 50000 chars. Fine.\n\nAlso every text creation requires the font loaded in that call. I'll create helper `T()` that awaits loadFontAsync cached in a Set.\n\nLet me draft helper prelude (compact):\n\n```js\nconst page = figma.root.children.find(p=>p.id==='5:2');\nawait figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page);\nconst C={pri:{r:.243,g:.322,b:.757},ink:{r:.09,g:.1,b:.12},sub:{r:.42,g:.44,b:.48},faint:{r:.65,g:.67,b:.71},wh:{r:1,g:1,b:1},sf:{r:.957,g:.961,b:.973},ln:{r:.906,g:.918,b:.937},grn:{r:.063,g:.722,b:.424},red:{r:.941,g:.267,b:.22},grnBg:{r:.898,g:.973,g:.937}? ...\n```\ncareful: grnBg {r:.898,g:.973,b:.949}.\n\nFonts map: R:'Regular',M:'Medium',S:'Semi Bold',B:'Bold'.\n```js\nconst F={R:'Regular',M:'Medium',S:'Semi Bold',B:'Bold'};\nconst loaded={};\nasync function ld(s){const f={family:'Inter',style:F[s]};if(!loaded[s]){await figma.loadFontAsync(f);loaded[s]=1;}return f;}\nasync function T(parent,str,size,w,color,x,y,op={}){const t=figma.createText();t.fontName=await ld(w);t.characters=str;t.fontSize=size;t.fills=[{type:'SOLID',color}];t.x=x;t.y=y;if(op.w){t.textAutoResize='NONE';t.resize(op.w,op.h||t.height);}if(op.align)t.textAlignHorizontal=op.align;if(op.lh)t.lineHeight={value:op.lh,unit:'PIXELS'};parent.appendChild(t);return t;}\nfunction R(parent,x,y,w,h,fill,r=0,op={}){const rc=figma.createRectangle();rc.x=x;rc.y=y;rc.resize(w,h);if(fill)rc.fills=[{type:'SOLID',color:fill}];else rc.fills=[];rc.cornerRadius=r;if(op.stroke){rc.strokes=[{type:'SOLID',color:op.stroke}];rc.strokeWeight=op.sw||1;}if(op.shadow)rc.effects=[{type:'DROP_SHADOW',color:{r:0.09,g:0.1,b:0.12,a:op.sa||0.06},offset:{x:0,y:op.sy||4},radius:op.sr||12,spread:0,visible:true,blendMode:'NORMAL'}];parent.appendChild(rc);return rc;}\nasync function I(id,parent,x,y,w,h){const n=await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id);const i=n.createInstance();parent.appendChild(i);i.x=x;i.y=y;if(w&&h){try{i.resize(w,h);}catch(e){}}return i;}\nfunction V(parent,points,color,x,y,sw=1.5){const v=figma.createVector();v.vectorPaths=[{windingRule:'NONE',data:points}];v.strokes=[{type:'SOLID',color}];v.strokeWeight=sw;v.fills=[];v.strokeCap='ROUND';v.strokeJoin='ROUND';v.x=x;v.y=y;parent.appendChild(v);return v;}\n```\n\nResize on instances: allowed. Aspect might distort logos — only resize preserving aspect: set width then height proportional? I'll resize to target height with same aspect: `const s=h/i.height; i.rescale? ` Instances have rescale? There's `rescale` on some nodes? There's `rescale(scale)` method on... `LayoutMixin`? Actually `rescale` exists on FrameNode etc. Simpler: compute scale and set width/height individually: `i.resize(i.width*s, i.height*s)`.\n\nText inside instances override: nested = inst.findOne(n=>n.type==='TEXT'&&n.name==='...') then set fontName (must load) then characters.\n\nNow components build call. Components placement: separate area at y=-700 or a \"Components\" frame in workspace at x=-1600? Put them in a frame \"StockWave Components\" at (0, -760) above screens? Or below at y=2000. I'll put at x=0,y=-700 row.\n\nTabBar component (375×84): bg white, top border line, 5 tabs each 75 wide: icon 24 + label 10px. Default state: Home active. Children names: \"Tab Home\", \"Tab Portfolio\", \"Tab Trade\", \"Tab Market\", \"Tab Profile\"; inside each: icon instance named \"Icon\" and text \"Label\". Trade: circle 44 primary with white icon centered vertically (overlapping top?) keep simple: icon in primary circle 40, label below.\n\nActive color override helper:\n```js\nasync function setTab(tb, name){\n  for(const tab of tb.children){\n    if(tab.type!=='FRAME'&&!tab.name.startsWith('Tab'))continue;\n    const on = tab.name==='Tab '+name;\n    const col = on?C.pri:C.faint;\n    const ic = tab.findOne(n=>n.type==='INSTANCE');\n    // tint all vector descendants\n    ...\n  }\n}\n```\nIcons are INSTANCE nodes (Icon_Home etc.) containing vectors. To tint: find all descendants with 'fills' and set to solid color. For Trade tab with primary circle: circle fill white→ when active? Keep Trade circle always primary, icon always white; label color changes.\n\nStockRow component (327×64): children: logo container (40×40 rounded surface + logo instance centered), name stack (ticker 15 S ink + company 12 R sub), sparkline vector, right stack (price 15 S ink right-aligned + change 12 S colored right). Names: \"Logo\",\"Ticker\",\"Company\",\"Spark\",\"Price\",\"Change\". Overrides: swap logo nested instance (swapComponent), texts, colors.\n\nFor F (Ford) no logo: fallback tile with letter \"F\" text. So StockRow's logo slot: a frame \"LogoBox\" 40×40 with child instance \"Logo\". For Ford: remove instance / hide it and add text \"F\". I'll implement: in override, delete the nested instance? Instances children can't be deleted. Can set visible=false. Then add text into LogoBox — can't add children to instance. Hmm. So instead make LogoBox not part of instance override problem: for Ford row, hide logo instance and draw fallback over it (absolutely positioned text on top of the row instance in the parent frame). Acceptable.\n\nWishRow component (327×56): LogoBox + Ticker + Company + Spark + Change (right). \n\nIndexCard (160×86): big ticker \"S&P 500\", sub \"S&P · Standard & Poor's\", change chip +49.50%.\n\nPayRow (327×72): icon box (Icon_Card), title \"Debit Card\", desc, radio circle (unchosen). Names: Icon, Title, Desc, Radio.\n\nProfileRow (327×52): title left, optional value right (\"English\"), chevron \"›\" text right.\n\nNumPad (375×264): 12 keys 4 rows ×3. Keys: text 24 R ink centered; delete key \"⌫\"; \"*\" key \"*\". Size: each key 125×66.\n\nOK. Also StatusBar & HomeIndicator instances per screen (from 4:180 / 4:191).\n\nScreen builder functions per call. Let me now also think about 02_Onboarding Blank_Phone_Frame (89×180) — scale up ~2x to ~180×364.\n\nSplash: symbol 44 → scale to 72; wordmark 127×25 → scale to 160×31. Center: symbol at y≈330, wordmark below.\n\nLet me write Call A: prelude + components creation. Then Call B: screens 01-03; Call C: 04-06; Call D: 07 (home, big); Call E: 08; Call F: 09; Call G: